---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '@/components/PageHeader.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/data/site';
import { url } from '@/lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Embedded panels — ind-ds"
  description="Ship ind-ds inside embedded panels: Chromium kiosks, Electron and Tauri shells and WebViews — with hardware constraints, offline bundles and local brokers in mind."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Use case"
    title="Embedded panels"
    lead="Many industrial screens are not open web pages but bundled applications: a Chromium kiosk, an Electron or Tauri shell, or a WebView on a panel device. ind-ds packages into any of these, with hardware limits and offline operation in mind."
    crumbs={[
      { label: 'Home', href: url('/') },
      { label: 'Use cases', href: url('/use-cases') },
      { label: 'Embedded panels' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container stack uc-detail">
      <h2>Typical environment</h2>
      <p>
        A dedicated device running a single application in kiosk mode — an embedded Chromium display on
        an industrial PC, an Electron or Tauri desktop shell, or a native app hosting a WebView.
        Hardware is often modest, storage is limited, and the panel may run for long periods without a
        reliable connection to any central system. A local MQTT broker on the machine or line is
        common.
      </p>

      <h2>User roles</h2>
      <p>
        A machine or line operator uses the panel as the primary interface; a commissioning engineer
        sets it up on site; an IT or OT team maintains the image and updates. Because the device is
        fixed and often unattended between uses, robustness and predictable startup matter more than
        flexibility.
      </p>

      <h2>Interface challenges</h2>
      <p>
        Rendering must stay smooth on constrained hardware, so heavy layouts and excessive animation
        are a liability. The app usually needs to boot into a known state and run offline, which means
        bundling assets locally rather than assuming a network. Touch input, fixed resolution and
        specific viewport sizes have to be respected, and where native widgets are used alongside the
        web layer, the two should still look like one product.
      </p>

      <h2>How ind-ds helps</h2>
      <p>
        Because the components are standard Web Components built on <code>@ind-ds/core</code>, they run
        in any Chromium-based shell without a framework requirement, and can be bundled for offline
        startup. The design tokens ship as CSS, JS, JSON and Dart, so a native panel that renders its
        own widgets can still share the exact colours, spacing and semantic states as the web layer —
        the token-only strategy. Touch-oriented components such as <code>ind-virtual-keyboard</code>,
        <code>ind-mode-selector</code> and <code>ind-start-stop-control</code> suit fixed panels, and
        <code>ind-connection-indicator</code> plus the <code>@ind-ds/mqtt</code> helpers work naturally
        against a local broker. The dark and high-contrast themes cover varied panel lighting.
      </p>

      <h2>Example composition</h2>
      <p>An offline kiosk panel might be described as:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Shell: an Electron, Tauri or embedded Chromium kiosk loading <code>@ind-ds/core</code> from a local bundle.</li>
        <li>Frame: <code>ind-app-header</code>, <code>ind-status-bar</code> and <code>ind-connection-indicator</code> against a local broker.</li>
        <li>Operation: <code>ind-mode-selector</code>, <code>ind-start-stop-control</code>, <code>ind-setpoint-control</code> and <code>ind-virtual-keyboard</code> for touch entry.</li>
        <li>Readouts: <code>ind-value</code>, <code>ind-gauge</code> and <code>ind-sparkline</code> for live state.</li>
        <li>Native peripherals styled from shared tokens so they match the web layer.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Components are named for illustration; none are rendered live on this page.</p>

      <h2>Integration approach</h2>
      <p>
        Describe these as strategies rather than shipped official bindings: browser apps; Electron or
        Tauri desktop via a WebView shell using <code>@ind-ds/core</code>; embedded Chromium kiosk
        panels; and Capacitor or Flutter WebView on mobile. Native stacks share tokens only — there is
        no official native component binding today. Bundle the components and assets for offline
        startup, and point the <code>@ind-ds/mqtt</code> helpers at a local broker where one is
        available, keeping <code>wss://</code> and broker authorization for anything beyond the device.
      </p>

      <h2>Adoption strategy</h2>
      <p>
        Prototype in a plain browser first, then move the same code into the shell you have chosen, so
        packaging is the only variable. Adopt tokens across any native peripherals early, and validate
        startup time, offline behaviour and touch response on the real target hardware rather than a
        development machine.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        The shells listed are integration strategies, not officially maintained ind-ds bindings; you own
        the packaging, update mechanism and device security. For native stacks, ind-ds shares tokens
        only — there is no native component library, so those widgets are yours to build. Offline
        operation depends on how you bundle and cache assets and on a local data source; the components
        render offline but cannot supply data the panel can no longer reach. ind-ds does not replace PLC
        programming, historian software or safety engineering.
      </p>

      <div class="panel uc-cta">
        <a href={url('/components')} class="btn btn--default">Explore components</a>
        <a href={url('/getting-started')} class="btn btn--ghost">Get started</a>
        <a href={SITE.storybook} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--ghost">Open Storybook →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .uc-detail h2 { margin-top: var(--ind-spacing-6); }
  .uc-detail p { max-width: 68ch; }
  .uc-detail ul { max-width: 68ch; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: var(--ind-spacing-2); }
  .uc-detail code { font-family: var(--ind-font-family-mono); font-size: 0.9em; }
  .uc-cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--ind-spacing-3); margin-top: var(--ind-spacing-8); padding: var(--ind-spacing-6); }
</style>
